    <div class="prose reveal">
      <h3>The story</h3>
      <p class="first">It began with a camera and a curiosity about people. No plan for a company, no thesis about content — just the belief that if you sit with someone long enough and ask the right question, what comes back is worth listening to.</p>
      <p>Over the next twelve years we told more than 7,000 stories. Ambition, love, loss, resilience, failure, success and everything in between. From a local train commuter to a global icon. From first-time founders to India's biggest names. From everyday lives to stories that made national headlines.</p>
      <p>What grew alongside them was a community of more than 7 million people — and an understanding of what makes someone stop, feel, share, and remember. <em>That understanding is the thing everything else was built on.</em></p>

      <h3>Beyond storytelling</h3>
      <p>Somewhere along the way the stories stopped only being read and started doing things. People found support. Businesses found customers. Causes found communities. Individuals found each other. Nearly ₹20 crore has been raised through the platform for individuals and social causes.</p>
      <p>That was the moment the work changed shape — when it became clear that a story with the right person on the other side of it is not content. It's leverage.</p>

      <h3>The evolution into The Human Company</h3>
      <p>Brands began coming to us — first for stories, then for audiences, then for content ecosystems and formats of their own. What started as a storytelling platform became a business spanning strategy, creative, production, original IPs and distribution.</p>
      <p>Humans of Bombay didn't get replaced by that. It became the flagship platform inside it — still telling stories, still the proving ground for every format we build.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

